## `imm` — an immutable reference

```
def print_list(list: List[Int]):
    print(list.__str__())
```

The function reads the original value rather than a copy, and cannot mutate it. {#0F766E}Passing is cheap by default: nothing is copied unless you ask.{/}

## The function takes exclusive ownership

```
def take_text(var text: String):
    text += "!"
```

The value is the function's to keep, mutate, or destroy. {#0F766E}`var` on an argument is ownership; `var` on a declaration is just how you declare a variable. Same keyword, two jobs.{/}

## A mutable reference can be the only reference

If a function receives a `mut` argument, it cannot also receive any other reference to that same value — mutable or immutable.

{#0F766E}This is what makes aliasing bugs unrepresentable, and it is why the compiler can optimize as if references never overlap.{/}

## Compile-time tracking of which variable a reference came from

An origin answers two questions: which variable owns the value, and may it be mutated through this reference.

{#0F766E}Origins are symbolic — the compiler tracks them, and nothing about them exists at run time.{/}

## `-> ref[origin] Type`

```
def __getitem__(
    ref self, index: Int
) raises -> ref[self] String:
    return self.names[index]
```

`ref[self]` says the returned reference lives as long as `self`. {#0F766E}At the call site, `ref name = list[2]` binds the reference; `var name = list[2]` takes a copy.{/}

## An origin union — `ref[a, b]`

```
def pick_one(
    cond: Bool, ref a: String, ref b: String
) -> ref[a, b] String:
    return a if cond else b
```

The union extends both lifetimes. {#0F766E}The result is mutable only if *both* origins are mutable — the weaker one wins.{/}

## `Movable` — structs conform by default

Moving a value is the baseline assumption; you no longer declare it.

{#0F766E}`Copyable` and `Deinitable` are still opt-in, so the default value is one you can move but not silently duplicate.{/}

## A default, no-op destructor

Most types get it without writing anything, and types that are just collections of other types rarely need to override `__deinit__`.

{#C62828}Renamed from `ImplicitlyDestructible`.{/}

## As soon as possible — after its last use

ASAP destruction runs after every sub-expression, using static analysis to find each value's last use.

{#0F766E}Not at end of scope, and not at a garbage collector's convenience. Destruction is a compile-time decision.{/}

## No — and it is not `Defaultable` either

{#C62828}`Array` is no longer `ImplicitlyCopyable` or `Defaultable`.{/}

Copying one is now something you ask for. An accidental full copy of a fixed-size buffer was too easy to write and too expensive to miss.

## One — `Pointer`

{#C62828}`Pointer` and `UnsafePointer` are unified.{/} The distinction moved from the type to the operations: anything that can go wrong is prefixed `unsafe_`.

{#0F766E}A pointer is no longer unsafe by name. Specific *operations* on it are.{/}

## An `Allocation` — an explicitly-destroyed handle

It holds the pointer *and* the layout used to allocate, and it must be passed to `dealloc(allocation^)`.

{#0F766E}Leaking it is a compile error rather than a slow leak, and `dealloc` already knows the size because the handle carried it.{/}

## Uninitialized — like a declared but unassigned variable

Dereferencing it before writing is undefined. You must initialize it first, and destroy the contents before deallocating.

{#0F766E}Track two things separately: the state of the pointer, and the state of the pointee.{/}

## `offset_from()`

Pointer subtraction gives the number of elements between them, not the number of bytes.

{#0F766E}Only meaningful for two pointers into the same allocation.{/}

## `Optional[Pointer[...]]` — `Pointer` is non-nullable

```
var ptr = Optional[Pointer[Int, MutUntrackedOrigin]]()
if ptr:
    var p = ptr.value()
```

{#0F766E}Nullability is opt-in and visible in the type, so a plain `Pointer` never needs a null check.{/}

## Single ownership of one heap item

The item is passed in when you initialize the pointer. An `OwnedPointer` can be moved but not copied, so there is exactly one owner and cleanup is automatic.

{#0F766E}Safe, non-array, and never uninitialized.{/}

## Shared ownership by reference count

It can be freely copied. Every instance shares a count, incremented on copy and decremented on destruction; the value dies when the count reaches zero.

{#0F766E}Reach for it when ownership genuinely is ambiguous — not to avoid thinking about who owns the value.{/}

## Naming which memory a pointer lives in

Generic address space for ordinary host memory, and distinct spaces for GPU global and shared memory.

{#0F766E}It is part of the pointer's type, so a shared-memory pointer cannot be silently passed where a global one is expected. `Span` carries the parameter too.{/}

## A pointer to memory Mojo did not allocate

It carries an untracked origin, because there is no Mojo variable whose lifetime could be tracked.

{#0F766E}The lifetime rules are then yours to enforce: the compiler has explicitly been told it cannot help.{/}

## In parentheses after the name

```
struct CapacitiveSensor(Copyable, DeflectionSensing):
    def fetch_reading(self) -> Float64:
        return Float64(21.5)
```

{#0F766E}There is no duck typing: conformance is declared, checked, and total. Partial conformance is not a thing.{/}

## No

Conformance is declared where the type is declared. There is no post-hoc extension and no way to retrofit a trait onto someone else's struct.

{#0F766E}Wrap the foreign type in your own struct and conform that.{/}

## `Some[Trait]`

```
def averaged_poll_2(
    sensor: Some[DeflectionSensing], samples: Int
) -> Float64:
    ...
```

Equivalent to declaring `[T: DeflectionSensing]` and taking a `T`. {#0F766E}Use it when the type name would appear exactly once.{/}

## Compile time from run time

Parameters go in `[]` and resolve at compile time; arguments go in `()` and resolve at run time.

{#0F766E}Mojo generates specialized code per concrete parameter, so generics cost nothing at run time.{/}

## No — always constrain or type it

Without a fixed set of required features the compiler has no guarantee about which operations are valid.

The most permissive bound is `AnyType`; `Deinitable` and `Writable` are common baselines. {#0F766E}The bound is the contract you get to rely on inside the body.{/}

## `where (condition, "message")`

The optional string literal is what the compiler prints when the constraint is not satisfied.

{#0F766E}A constraint diagnostic is read by someone who cannot see your implementation. Spend a sentence on it.{/}

## `TypeList.all_conforms_to()`

It proves the property for the whole pack at once and refines the elements, so each one can be used through that trait inside the body.

{#0F766E}The alternative is checking element by element, which the compiler cannot then generalize.{/}

## The compiler warns about the redundancy

Composing a trait with one that already refines it adds nothing, and 1.0 says so rather than silently accepting it.

{#0F766E}Usually the sign that a refinement relationship exists that you can lean on instead.{/}

## No single one — the final trait set replaced it

{#C62828}`CollectionElement` is gone.{/} Requirements are now stated as exactly the traits a container needs: `Copyable`, `Movable`, `Deinitable`, and so on.

{#0F766E}Its problem was bundling: it demanded capabilities most containers never needed.{/}

## File I/O, foreign calls, and anything that can raise

Compile-time evaluation runs real Mojo code, but not code that reaches outside the compiler or that can fail.

{#0F766E}If a `comptime` value won't fold, one of these three is usually why.{/}

## Making a compile-time value available at run time

Implicitly copyable values such as `Int` and `Bool` materialize automatically when assigned to a runtime variable.

{#0F766E}The compile-time and run-time worlds are separate; materialization is the one-way door between them.{/}

## `field_count()`, `field_names()`, `field_types()`

They return the count, the names, and the types — enough to drive a `comptime for` that generates code shaped to the struct.

{#0F766E}This is how one serializer covers every struct without any of them knowing about it.{/}

## Newly introduced and incomplete, by the docs' own admission

{#C62828}Expect gaps and expect it to change.{/}

{#0F766E}Fine for code generation you control and can recompile. A poor foundation for a published API.{/}

## The ternary type expression

A type can be selected inline with an ordinary conditional expression, so choosing between two types no longer needs a dedicated helper.

{#C62828}`ConditionalType` is gone.{/}

## A type with some parameters left unspecified

Written with `_` in the parameter position, it names a family rather than one type — the same underscore that leaves an origin unbound in `ref[_]`.

{#0F766E}It lets a signature say "a `Span` over anything" without inventing a parameter to bind.{/}

## Tells the compiler two types are the same when it cannot see it

In a branch where a constraint has already established the equality, `rebind` converts between the two spellings.

{#0F766E}It asserts a compile-time fact — it is not a cast and does not convert anything at run time.{/}

## When it is marked infer-only with `//`

```
def foo[T: Intable, //](x: T) -> Int:
    return x.__int__()
```

Everything after `//` is deduced from the arguments and cannot be given explicitly. {#0F766E}This is how higher-order functions take a function's type.{/}

## Field handles were renamed for 1.0

{#C62828}Beta-era field-handle spellings no longer resolve.{/} Use the `reflect[T]` surface — `field`, `field_ref`, `field_offset` — as documented.

{#0F766E}Reflection code is the most likely thing in a beta codebase to need rewriting by hand.{/}

## `None`

It does not infer from the body. A lambda written for its side effect therefore needs no annotation, and one that returns a value must say so.

{#0F766E}A lambda that silently returns nothing is the likeliest first surprise.{/}

## A function bundled with values from its surrounding scope

The capture list — the braces after the argument list — is what makes the distinction syntactic rather than inferred.

{#0F766E}You can see from the signature whether state came along.{/}

## Free variables are captured immutably

The default is `imm`, so a lambda written without braces can read its surroundings but not modify them.

{#0F766E}The quiet part: it is still a closure, and still carries state.{/}

## It has storage, so it is a runtime value

Capturing makes the lambda a type with both behavior and storage — an instance, not a function type.

{#0F766E}This is the practical dividing line: a capture-free lambda can be a parameter, a capturing one has to be an argument.{/}

## By explicit trait conformance

{#C62828}Conformance is no longer implicit.{/} A closure states which closure traits it satisfies, and a higher-order API states which it requires.

{#0F766E}The effect a function may have — raising, capturing — is part of its type.{/}

## Ordinary lambdas and closures with capture lists

{#C62828}The legacy closure decorators are deprecated{/}, along with `@parameter if` and `@parameter for`, which became `comptime if` and `comptime for`.

{#0F766E}Capture behaviour is now written in the capture list instead of implied by a decorator.{/}

## Yes — new in 1.0

A function value with unbound parameters can be called, with the parameters supplied at the call rather than baked in when the pointer was formed.

{#0F766E}One pointer can then serve several specializations.{/}

## `Scalar[DType.int]`

The builtin integer is a one-element SIMD vector, which is why scalars and vectors share operators and methods.

{#0F766E}`Scalar[dtype]` is `SIMD[dtype, 1]`.{/}

## Typing a parameter that is a SIMD vector length

```
def frob[w: SIMDLength](v: SIMD[DType.int, w]):
    ...
```

New in 1.0. {#0F766E}A plain `Int` would accept lengths that are not valid vector widths; `SIMDLength` will not.{/}

## Everything, not just `SIMD`

{#C62828}Renamed throughout the library.{/} `SIMD`'s second parameter, `List.resize(new_length=)`, `ComplexSIMD[..., length=N]`, and `Array`'s parameter.

{#0F766E}One name per concept — `size` had meant both element count and byte count.{/}

## `SIMD[DType.bool, length]` — a mask, not a `Bool`

The comparison is elementwise, so the result is one boolean per lane.

{#0F766E}Use a reduction, or a select, to turn a mask into a decision.{/}

## A reduction

Sum, min and max fold the lanes into a scalar.

{#0F766E}A reduction is a synchronization point in vector code: everything before it can stay in lanes, everything after is scalar.{/}

## Atomic read-modify-write on a scalar value

From `std.atomic`, with an `Ordering` type controlling memory ordering between threads.

{#0F766E}The intended uses are lock-free data structures, reference counting, and synchronization primitives — not ordinary shared state.{/}

## A standalone memory barrier

`std.atomic.fence` orders memory operations without being attached to any particular atomic access.

{#0F766E}Reach for it when the ordering you need is between *operations*, not on one variable.{/}

## Because the default — full ordering — is the expensive one

`Ordering` lets you ask for exactly the guarantee the algorithm needs, so the compiler and CPU keep the reorderings that are still safe.

{#C62828}Weakening an ordering you have not reasoned about is how lock-free code goes wrong.{/}

## Removed

{#C62828}The sentinel is gone.{/} A `DType` parameter is constrained to the dtypes that are actually valid, rather than carrying a value meaning "none of them".

{#0F766E}Express absence with `Optional`, not with an in-band sentinel.{/}

## Low-precision floats and wide integers

Alongside `Float32` and `Float64`: `BFloat16` and `Float8_e4m3fn`, plus integers from `Int8` through `Int256` and their unsigned counterparts.

{#0F766E}The narrow float formats are there for accelerator work, where they are the native storage type.{/}
